  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1046 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nTuesday night through Monday:\n\nLatest models seem to be coming into better agreement with the\nmid-week system. There is expected to be a prolonged period of\nhigher moisture across the region, which will move in Tuesday \nnight and exit during the day Thursday. Precipitable water \nvalues are likely to peak in the 1.5 to 2.0 inch range during \nthis period, which will be near or above max for this time of \nthe year per GRB and DVN sounding climatology. While overall \ninstability and wind profiles are not supportive of a severe \nweather threat mid-week, the high precipitable water values and\nmarginal instability could result in locally heavy rainfall. \n\nThe main area of uncertainty mid-week is with the timing and \nplacement of the stronger forcing, with generally persistent \nwarm air and moisture advection expected. A few model solutions\nplace the higher precip totals mainly to the northwest of the \nforecast area Tue night through Wed night, shifting the higher \ntotals to the south by Thursday afternoon as the trough and cold\nfront drop through the area. The ECMWF solution remains the \nmain holdout on bringing precip into southern Wisconsin earlier\nand then keeping the better forcing across the forecast area \nfor a longer period of time. Overall, rain chances will be high \nfor mid-week given the high amount of moisture, but timing and \ntotals remain uncertain at this time. Temperatures should drop \nback to around normal for Wed/Thu given the cloud and rain \nchances.\n\nHigh pressure will build in Friday behind the mid-week system, \nbringing dry weather and temperatures just a touch below normal\nfor Friday and Saturday. A shortwave and cold front are then\nexpected to slide through Saturday night, with strong high\npressure building into the region Sunday and Monday. It's\ncurrently looking dry with the frontal passage this weekend,\nwith even cooler conditions likely early next week. \n\nDDV",
